A '''chemical compound''' is a [[chemical substance]] composed of many identical [[molecule]]s (or [[molecular entity|molecular entities]]) containing [[atom]]s from more than one [[chemical element]] held together by [[chemical bond]]s. A molecule consisting of atoms of only one element, called ''[[homonuclear molecule]]'', is therefore not a compound. A compound can be transformed into a different substance by a [[chemical reaction]], which may involve interactions with other substances. In this process, bonds between atoms may be broken or new bonds formed or both.

There are four major types of compounds, distinguished by how the constituent atoms are bonded together. [[Molecular compound]]s are held together by [[covalent bond]]s, [[ionic compound]]s are held together by [[ionic bond]]s, [[intermetallic]] compounds are held together by [[metallic bond]]s, and [[coordination complex]]es are held together by [[coordinate covalent bond]]s. [[Non-stoichiometric compound]]s form a disputed marginal case.

A [[chemical formula]] specifies the number of atoms of each element in a compound molecule, using the standard [[chemical symbol]]s with numerical [[subscript]]s. Many chemical compounds have a unique [[CAS number]] identifier assigned by the [[Chemical Abstracts Service]]. Globally, more than 350,000 chemical compounds (including mixtures of chemicals) have been registered for production and use.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang|first1=Zhanyun|last2=Walker|first2=Glen W.|last3=Muir|first3=Derek C. G.|last4=Nagatani-Yoshida|first4=Kakuko|date=2020-01-22|title=Toward a Global Understanding of Chemical Pollution: A First Comprehensive Analysis of National and Regional Chemical Inventories|journal=[[Environmental Science & Technology]]|volume=54|issue=5|pages=2575–2584|doi=10.1021/acs.est.9b06379|pmid=31968937|bibcode=2020EnST...54.2575W|doi-access=free|hdl=20.500.11850/405322|hdl-access=free}}</ref>

{{short description|History of the Python programming language}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=August 2026}}
{{use American English|date=May 2024}}
[[File:Python logo 1990s.svg|thumb|Old Python logo, 1990s–2006]]
[[File:Python logo and wordmark.svg|alt=|thumb|New Python logo, 2006–present]]
[[File:Guido-portrait-2014.jpg|thumb|Guido van Rossum in 2014]]

The [[programming language]] [[Python (programming language)|Python]] was conceived in the late 1980s,<ref name="venners-interview-pt-1">{{cite web |title=The Making of Python |url=http://www.artima.com/intv/pythonP.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160901183332/http://www.artima.com/intv/pythonP.html |archive-date=September 1, 2016 |access-date=March 22, 2007 |publisher=Artima Developer}}</ref> and its implementation was started in December 1989<ref name="timeline-of-python" /> by [[Guido van Rossum]] at [[Centrum Wiskunde & Informatica|CWI]] in [[the Netherlands]] as a successor to [[ABC (programming language)|ABC]] capable of [[exception handling]] and interfacing with the [[Amoeba (operating system)|Amoeba operating system]].<ref name="faq-created">{{cite web |title=Why Was Python Created in the First Place? |url=https://www.python.org/doc/faq/general/#why-was-python-created-in-the-first-place |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080223222507/http://www.python.org/doc/faq/general/#why-was-python-created-in-the-first-place |archive-date=February 23, 2008 |access-date=March 22, 2007 |publisher=Python FAQ}}</ref> Van Rossum was Python's principal author and had a central role in deciding the direction of Python (as reflected in the title given to him by the Python community, [[Benevolent dictator for life|''Benevolent Dictator for Life'' (BDFL)]]<ref>{{cite web |last=van Rossum |first=Guido |date=July 31, 2008 |title=Origin of BDFL |url=http://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread=235725 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110116210946/http://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread=235725 |archive-date=January 16, 2011 |access-date=August 1, 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=March 7, 2006 |title=Python Creator Scripts Inside Google |url=http://www.eweek.com/c/a/Application-Development/Python-Creator-Scripts-Inside-Google/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20180809122951/http://www.eweek.com/development/python-creator-scripts-inside-google |archive-date=August 9, 2018 |access-date=May 13, 2008 |publisher=www.eweek.com}}</ref>) until stepping down as leader on July 12, 2018.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fairchild |first1=Carlie |date=July 12, 2018 |title=Guido Van Rossum Stepping Down from Role as Python's Benevolent Dictator for Life |url=https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/guido-van-rossum-stepping-down-role-pythons-benevolent-dictator-life |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180713192427/https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/guido-van-rossum-stepping-down-role-pythons-benevolent-dictator-life |archive-date=July 13, 2018 |access-date=July 12, 2018 |work=Linux Journal |language=en}}</ref> Python was named after the [[BBC TV]] show ''[[Monty Python's Flying Circus]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=General Python FAQ — Python 3.8.3 Documentation |url=https://docs.python.org/3/faq/general.html#why-is-it-called-python |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121024164224/http://docs.python.org/faq/general.html#why-is-it-called-python |archive-date=October 24, 2012 |access-date=June 4, 2020 |website=docs.python.org}}</ref>

Python 2.0 was released on October 16, 2000, with many major new features, such as [[list comprehension]]s, [[cycle detection|cycle-detecting]] [[garbage collection (computer science)|garbage collector]], [[reference counting]], [[memory management]] and support for [[Unicode]], along with a change to the development process itself, with a shift to a more transparent and community-backed process.<ref name="newin-2.0">{{cite web |last1=Kuchling |first1=Andrew M. |last2=Zadka |first2=Moshe |title=What's New in Python 2.0 |url=http://www.amk.ca/python/2.0/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091214142515/http://www.amk.ca/python/2.0 |archive-date=December 14, 2009 |access-date=March 22, 2007}}</ref>

Python 3.0, a major, backwards-incompatible release, was released on December 3, 2008<ref name="3.0-release">{{cite web |title=Welcome to Python.org |url=https://www.python.org/download/releases/3.0/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200614153714/https://www.python.org/download/releases/3.0/ |archive-date=June 14, 2020 |access-date=December 27, 2016 |work=python.org}}</ref> after a long period of testing. Many of its major features were also [[backport]]ed to the backwards-compatible Python versions 2.6 and 2.7<ref name="pep-3000" /> until support for Python 2 finally ceased at the beginning of 2020. Releases of Python&nbsp;3 up through 3.12 include the <code>2to3</code> utility, which automates the translation of Python&nbsp;2 code to Python&nbsp;3.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2to3 — Automated Python 2 to 3 Code Translation — Python 3.12.12 Documentation |url=https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/2to3.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251212075917/https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/2to3.html |archive-date=December 12, 2025 |access-date=December 12, 2025 |website=docs.python.org}}</ref>

{{As of|October 2026}}, Python 3.15 <!-- not sure why not displaying: {{wikidata|property|P548=Q2804309|P348}} --> is the latest stable release. This version currently receives full bug-fix and security updates, while Python 3.14—released in October 2025—will continue to receive bug-fixes until October 2027, and after that will only receive security fixes until its end-of-life in 2030. Python 3.11 is the oldest supported branch of Python (albeit in the 'security support' phase).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Status of Python Versions |url=https://devguide.python.org/versions/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220826132905/https://devguide.python.org/versions/ |archive-date=August 26, 2022 |access-date=October 7, 2024 |website=Python Developer's Guide |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=October 8, 2024 |title=Python |url=https://endoflife.date/python |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210918162455/https://endoflife.date/python |archive-date=September 18, 2021 |access-date=November 20, 2024 |website=endoflife.date |language=en-US}}</ref>

