[[File:People-World-Map-Prismatic-2.png|thumb|Social technology contributes to a networked society.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Social Technologies and Collective Intelligence|last=Skaržauskienė|first=Aelita|publisher=Mykolas Romeris University|year=2015|isbn=9789955197089}}</ref>]]
'''Social technology''' is a way of using human, intellectual and digital resources in order to influence social processes.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328880757|title=Socialinių technologijų taikymo galimybės gyventojų dalyvavimui viešojo valdymo sprendimų priėmimo procesuose|last=Tamošiūnaitė|first=Rūta|publisher=Mykolas Romeris University|year=2018|isbn=978-9955-19-904-5|location=Vilnius, Lituania|pages=11}}</ref> For example, one might use social technology to ease social procedures via social software and social hardware, which might include the use of computers and [[information technology]] for governmental procedures or business practices. It has historically referred to two meanings: as a term related to [[social engineering (political science)|social engineering]], a meaning that began in the 19th century, and as a description of [[social software]], a meaning that began in the early 21st century.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Leibeseder|first=Bettina|date=January 2011|title=A CRITICAL REVIEW ON THE CONCEPT OF SOCIAL TECHNOLOGY|url=https://www.mruni.eu/en/mokslo_darbai/st/archyvas/?l=111159|journal=Socialines Technologijos/Social Technology|pages=7–24}}</ref> Social technology is also split between human-oriented technologies and artifact-oriented technologies.<ref name=":1" />

{{Short description|American non-profit research and development organization}}
{{coord|display=title|42.01995|-87.92241|type:landmark}}
The '''Gas Technology Institute''' ('''GTI''') is an [[Americans|American]] non-profit [[research and development]] organization which develops, demonstrates, and licenses new energy technologies for private and public clients, with a particular focus on the [[natural gas]] industry.  GTI is located in [[Des Plaines, Illinois]].

{{Short description|English expression}}
[[File:GodfreyKneller-IsaacNewton-1689.jpg|thumb|[[Isaac Newton]] whose Principia Mathematica established physics as a model of mathematical precision that other disciplines have sought to emulate]]
The term '''physics envy''' is used to criticize the modern writing and research of academics working in the "[[soft sciences|softer sciences]]", such as [[economics]], [[sociology]], [[psychology]], [[political science]], or [[anthropology]].<ref name="Overcoming ‘Physics Envy’">{{cite news|last1=Clarke|first1=Kevin|last2=Primo|first2=David|title=Overcoming 'Physics Envy'|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/04/01/opinion/sunday/the-social-sciences-physics-envy.html|newspaper=[[New York Times]]|date=31 March 2012|accessdate=10 August 2016}}</ref><ref name="Sokal">{{cite web|last1=Sokal|first1=Alan|title=Physics envy in psychology: A cautionary tale|url=http://www.physics.nyu.edu/sokal/CCNY_lecture_Nov_19_15.pdf|publisher=[[New York University]]|accessdate=10 August 2016}}</ref><ref name="How Business Schools Lost Their Way">{{cite journal|last1=Bennis|first1=Warren|last2=O'Toole|first2=James|title=How Business Schools Lost Their Way|url=https://hbr.org/2005/05/how-business-schools-lost-their-way|journal=[[Harvard Business Review]]|date=May 2005|accessdate=10 August 2016}}</ref> The term argues that writing and working practices in these disciplines have overused confusing [[jargon]] and complicated mathematics to seem more rigorous, predictable or quantifiable as in heavily [[mathematics]]-based [[natural science]] subjects like [[physics]].<ref name="Dunbar2011">{{cite book|author=Robin Dunbar|title=The Trouble with Science|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5HbvQsET6zIC&pg=PA214|date=7 April 2011|publisher=Faber & Faber|isbn=978-0-571-26519-0|pages=214–231}}</ref><ref name="Academic B.S. as artificial barriers to entry">{{cite web|last1=Smith|first1=Noah|title=Academic B.S. as artificial barriers to entry|url=http://noahpinionblog.blogspot.co.uk/2015/12/academic-bs-as-artificial-barriers-to.html|website=Economics, neologisms, and distraction|date=12 December 2015|accessdate=10 August 2016}}</ref>
